<?nvs
namespace Registry;

use Core\Html\Markup;

// The page that says what the operator of the registry may do with a published version
// (`rule:packaging/the-operator-only-withholds`). The text is the same for every request.
final class PolicyPage
{
    public static function page(): Markup
    {
        return PackagePage::layout('What the operator may do', html`<h1>What the operator of this registry may do</h1>
<p>The operator is the person or group that runs this registry. The operator can do one thing to a published version: withhold it. The operator does this only for malware or for a legal order.</p>
<p>A withheld version keeps its name, its version number and its hash in the index and in the log. Only its files are no longer served. <code>nvs</code> does not install a withheld version, and it prints the reason.</p>
<p>Every withhold is an advisory. An advisory is a signed entry in the public log of the registry, and it states the reason. So every withhold is public, and anybody can check it.</p>
<p>The website has no account for the operator. The operator can withhold a version only from the server itself.</p>
<h2>What the operator never does</h2>
<ul>
<li>Delete a version.</li>
<li>Change the files of a version.</li>
<li>Give a package name to another account.</li>
<li>Check packages before they are published.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Why</h2>
<p>A deleted version breaks every project that uses it, and nobody can see why. A name that goes to another account changes the code behind every project that uses that name. A check before publishing makes every release wait, and no check can promise that a package is safe.</p>
<p>Withholding is enough for malware and for a legal order. Because it is public and signed, you can check each time it is used.</p>`);
    }
}
